 ## Background
 Kernel-based resampling is a fundamental technique in digital signal processing and computer graphics, rooted in the Whittaker–Shannon interpolation formula which states that a band-limited signal can be perfectly reconstructed from its samples using a sinc kernel [G]. In volume rendering, exact sinc reconstruction is computationally prohibitive due to its infinite support, leading to the development of truncated and windowed kernels (such as Lanczos) and polynomial approximations (such as B-splines) [G]. The method is widely used in direct volume ray casting to ensure smooth visual transitions and accurate gradient estimation for lighting calculations [G].
